ronment could move Virginia meaningfully toward either side.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Virginia enters the 2028 presidential race as a modest Democratic-leaning battleground. The state has backed the Democratic nominee in recent presidential cycles, and its partisan lean sits about three points on the Democratic side of the ledger. With the incumbent president term-limited, neither party benefits from incumbency, and the national environment is treated as neutral for now.\n\nNo general-election polls, expert ratings, or market prices are available yet, so the forecast rests on fundamentals alone. That keeps uncertainty wide: a three-point Democratic edge is well within the range where either party could win. Recent coverage has centered on the Democratic primary calendar and Virginia's early primary date rather than the fall contest, offering little signal about the general election. Democrats' 2025 statewide victories, including the governor's office, provide a favorable recent benchmark, but the presidential race remains a genuine tossup with a slight Democratic tilt.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6351, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6229, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6055, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6063, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6125, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6095, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6104, "margin": 3.0}]}